mething that's going  to go away there are five million people  now waiting for procedures and  operations as a direct result of kovid  that would rise to about 13 million by  the end of this year if we did nothing  so actually to do nothing isn't an  option so yes of course there's a lot to  be worked out in my view i would like to  see an insurance system for for social  care to top it up for people to be able  to insure their own assets and so on but  in the short term we have to do  something to deal with the covid backlog  and to sort out this social care system  that's already broken can you say a bit  more about that insurance system lots of  other countries do you have that it  seems to be an option do you think  there's potential to develop that  yeah i do actually and i was interested  yesterday the prime minister was saying  there'll be a white paper about social  care coming forward i mean i personally  having uh presided at defra over the  establishment of floodry which you might  recall was when we had those terrible  floods and people couldn't get flood  insurance so the government set up a  quasi private sector with government  backing system for flood insurance where  every household of bandy council tax or  higher had 10 pounds a year paid out of  their council tax into a pot that the  government then added to  to enable the private sector to agree to  ensure households against floods so that  kind of socializing this system but  bringing in the private sector to  actually be able to  provide some backing for it i think then  you could have a system where people  were paying insurance premiums and  potentially the older you are the richer  you are the much greater your insurance  premium would be as is the case with  private health care so i think a mixture  of um state and private sector would be  a good way to go but i think what the  government's done now is to get a grip  on something that is already broken so i  think it's absolutely essential that we  do that right what the government's done  now otherwise you say is give a lot of  money to nhs with a huge backlog but  interesting that you're saying that  insurance works for flood insurance  insurance work for floods so why  couldn't insurance work for long-term  social care as it does in other  countries isn't is it could could this  be the trick the government has missed  or do you think there's still the  potential to um to arrive at something  like this before you get into the phase  where the money is transferred from the  nhs and goes towards the care home  sector  so i think that there's two aspects to  this one is we have a system that's  broken right now so we've got this huge  backlog in the nhs and we have a social  care system where people can't get  domiciliary care the the care home the  quality of care homes and the  availability of care homes for people is  simply not there so we have a short-term  problem that we really need to fix and i  do think that biting the bullet and you  know facing up to the fact that it's  breaking a manifesto promise and so on  people will be relieved that the  government's getting a grip on it  however in the longer term i think  people who say well how do we know that  this is going to be sufficient is a very  valid question to ask so yes i would  like to see us looking at a longer term  flood re-style of  national insurance scheme that people  could buy into  andrea thanks very much indeed i'm now  joined by marcus fish who's been one of  the more outspoken rebels against what  the prime minister has announced marcus  how would you describe the reaction  amongst your fellow tories to the fairly  big announcement this week  well i intend to keep my manifesto  pledge  to not increase  these taxes  that are proposed national insurance i  think is the wrong  measure  um at the wrong time  um and i think it hits the wrong  people to be paying for something  um which we should be thinking of in a  different way and funding a different  way right you've been quite forthright  in that you describe it as a socialist  approach a big mistake it's um you don't  believe it's conservatives can you say a  bit more about that what's  unconservative about this  well what's unconservative is to  reach for tax levers at the first  instance to  to pay for costs which are temporary on  the economy in terms of the nhs recovery  from covid  and i think you know tax and spend is  what people would normally expect from  the other side of the aisle rather than  us but nobody expects a global pandemic  and you'll accept the costs of fixing  the backlog aren't what any tory could  put of a normal gamut surely these are  exceptional and policies for exceptional  times  i think we should make a distinction  between  costs  that are one-off that are not going to  recur because of the pandemic and  costs of the ongoing and recurring  need to help  our burgeoning older population with its  health and social care  needs and i think it's been a bit  disingenuous to  for the government to be mixing the two  things up  in the way that they are with this uh  very broad base and  in my view uh it will become  irreversible  tax rise now marcus in the last few days  i've spoken to quite a few tory mps who  say what you say but they're and  disturbed by this some say they're  sickened and appalled at the idea of  increasing the um the tax on somebody on  the minimum wage to subsidize somebody  with a two million pound house  the funny thing is that the tories will  complain about this but they won't vote  against it even you often voted against  it you've abstained so why is it that so  many of your colleagues i'm sure you've  had people saying to you marcus i agree  with what you're saying in public but  they themselves will not join you in  public criticism there's a sort of  fatalism about this i'm beginning to  wonder if the tory party is actually far  less opposed to this than a lot of the  backbenchers make out they are  there is very broad unhappiness about  what has been proposed and the way it's  been proposed i think people do think  it's it's unfair broadly um on the  working age population  to be taxed in this way and at risk to  their jobs  and their employers who are trying to  get back on their feet after the  pandemic i think they think it's both  politically risky and economically risky  but the reality is that we  need to give the government space to be  able to  come forward with the full proposals and  try to work with them to  get the policies changed and improved  and that's why  i think it's right  not to have voted against the government  at this stage so that we can at least  have a  constructive conversation with them  going forward and try to  fix what i think is a  uh  a misguided policy that's been done more  for sort of pr reasons  uh than for sound  policy reasons but boris johnson seems  to have  certainly got a warm reaction to this  the opinion poll suggests that voters  certainly older voters seem to think  he's made a sensible decision they  recognize that in the pandemic taxis  need to go up and a lot of people want  their care homes fixed i mean when  theresa may came up with the idea of  asking the wealthy to pay for the cost  of their own care the tory's got such a  backlash that you guys lost your  majority so i put it to you that's what  you're proposing just simply isn't  politically sellable now given what the  political power electoral power lies  where it does with older voters because  without older voters the tories would  not be in power so you guys basically  work for them from now on  i don't think that this  plan  will actually fix social care i think it  will make it worse  and i think  the people i've talked to in the care  sector and local government are  extremely worried that in fact this tax  rise money will just be taken by the nhs  and it will disappear they will never  give it up  and in the meantime  the industry has to cope with the  changes that the government has  announced  to mean for example that um  uh care providers can't cross-subsidize  their state uh funded provision with the  private uh care care residents  um and that means that many of them will  be uneconomic there'll be less capacity  in the industry in the next two years  which will make it harder to fix the nhs  with the discharge  requirements that are there to  get all the work and the backlog cleared  and i just think this is an absolute  mess of a policy um that hasn't been  properly thought through  and i think it's  basically a bit of a wheeze that someone  has thought up to use what you  mentioned which is the very good will of  the british people to  want to  to help this system which has clearly  been overloaded with lots of demand  because of the pandemic  and  forced through a tax rise um but call it  something else call it a goodwill  payment to the nhs which  people like to say that they want more  health care and they'll pay for it and  all the rest of it but face them with a  tax rise that could  interfere with the economic recovery and  let's see if they really want to pay for  that  [Music]  you
 

